<p class="dossier-text">
  <strong>Jovienne Maren Calder.</strong> Born March 14, 1982, in Carrow, Illinois. Population 2,800. Church suppers, high school football, everybody knows your name. The kind of place where reputation is currency and Prosper Calder was the richest man in town. Middle child. Older brother Hadrien. Younger sister Solène. A house with a porch and a backyard. A father who coached Little League and organized the church pancake breakfast.
</p>

<p class="dossier-text">
  The abuse began when she was seven. It continued until she was twelve. She told her mother. Her mother's face closed like a door. She told a counselor. The counselor called Prosper. She told a teacher. The teacher looked at Prosper Calder — church deacon, everyone's favorite father — and looked at Jovienne — small, angry, already labeled <em>troubled</em> — and the math was done. Nobody believed her. Not once. Not ever.
</p>

<p class="dossier-text">
  She made a crossroads deal in April 2007. Traded her soul for one thing: her father's death. The demon offered ten years. She said <em>one.</em> Three days later, Prosper Calder died. Heart attack. The church was standing-room-only at the funeral. Jovienne wore black and felt nothing but the clean, bright absence of fear.
</p>

<p class="dossier-text">
  She died in April 2008. She spent forty to fifty years in Hell. She held out longer than most — not because she was strong, but because the worst thing that had ever happened to her happened in a house with floral wallpaper, and Hell was at least <em>honest</em> about what it was. She broke. She picked up the blade. She became what they wanted, and what frightened her most was how easy it was.
</p>

<p class="dossier-text">
  In October 2008, she woke in a field outside Pontiac, Illinois. Body intact. No memory of extraction. No scars from her previous life. She had one objective: <strong>find Dean Winchester.</strong>
</p>

<div class="pull">
  "You don't get to give up. You told me I was real. Prove it."
  <cite>5x18, "point of no return"</cite>
</div>

